WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:02.720
Welcome to Materials Science.

2
00:00:02.720 --> 00:00:05.653
In this first topic we answer a simple question.

3
00:00:05.653 --> 00:00:09.493
What actually is a material, and why should an engineer care?

4
00:00:10.313 --> 00:00:12.437
Your lecture draws a clear line.

5
00:00:12.437 --> 00:00:16.466
Oils, gases and pharmaceuticals are not materials in our sense.

6
00:00:16.466 --> 00:00:19.352
Iron, copper, polymers and cement are.

7
00:00:19.773 --> 00:00:22.307
The difference is that a material can be put into a

8
00:00:22.307 --> 00:00:25.488
geometric shape, and the product then has a function.

9
00:00:25.908 --> 00:00:30.718
And civilizations are named after their materials: the stone age, the bronze

10
00:00:30.718 --> 00:00:35.929
age, the iron age, and now the information age, built on semiconductors.

11
00:00:39.629 --> 00:00:44.164
Two terms to keep separate. Materials science studies the relationships between

12
00:00:44.164 --> 00:00:47.012
the structure of a material and its properties.

13
00:00:47.432 --> 00:00:50.076
Materials engineering goes the other way.

14
00:00:50.076 --> 00:00:55.089
It designs the structure of a material to give it a predetermined set of properties.

15
00:00:55.508 --> 00:00:58.967
So the scientist asks why a material behaves the way it does,

16
00:00:58.967 --> 00:01:02.160
and the engineer asks how to make it do what we need.

17
00:01:05.860 --> 00:01:09.892
A property is the response of a material to an external stimulus.

18
00:01:10.312 --> 00:01:15.543
Pull on it, and the mechanical response is deformation. Heat it, and it expands.

19
00:01:15.543 --> 00:01:17.902
Apply a voltage, and current flows.

20
00:01:18.321 --> 00:01:21.139
Your lecture lists five families: mechanical,

21
00:01:21.139 --> 00:01:24.170
thermal, electrical, magnetic and optical.

22
00:01:24.170 --> 00:01:28.265
Callister adds a sixth, deteriorative, which covers corrosion.

23
00:01:28.686 --> 00:01:30.706
One key point from the slide.

24
00:01:30.706 --> 00:01:34.522
Properties are independent of the shape and size of the part.

25
00:01:34.522 --> 00:01:36.767
Stiffness is a material property.

26
00:01:36.767 --> 00:01:39.348
How far a particular beam bends is not.

27
00:01:43.047 --> 00:01:45.052
The whole course follows one chain.

28
00:01:45.052 --> 00:01:48.156
Processing, structure, properties and performance.

29
00:01:48.576 --> 00:01:50.501
Processing sets the structure.

30
00:01:50.501 --> 00:01:52.790
The structure decides the properties.

31
00:01:52.790 --> 00:01:56.223
And the properties decide how the part performs in service.

32
00:01:56.643 --> 00:02:00.576
Here is the example from your lecture: jet engine turbine blades.

33
00:02:00.576 --> 00:02:03.422
At high temperature, grain boundaries creep.

34
00:02:03.842 --> 00:02:07.838
Going from equiaxed grains, to columnar grains, to a single

35
00:02:07.838 --> 00:02:11.893
crystal with no grain boundaries at all, let designers raise the

36
00:02:11.893 --> 00:02:15.829
operating temperature by about two hundred degrees Celsius.

37
00:02:19.929 --> 00:02:23.726
Now the classes. Solids are grouped into metals, ceramics and

38
00:02:23.726 --> 00:02:26.626
polymers, plus composites that combine them.

39
00:02:27.046 --> 00:02:32.237
Metals, like iron, copper and aluminum, have large numbers of free electrons.

40
00:02:32.237 --> 00:02:37.211
That makes them good conductors of heat and electricity, strong, and ductile.

41
00:02:37.631 --> 00:02:42.849
Ceramics are compounds of metals and nonmetals, like oxides, nitrides and carbides.

42
00:02:42.849 --> 00:02:47.534
They are stiff, strong and very hard, but brittle, and they insulate.

43
00:02:47.954 --> 00:02:53.216
Polymers, like polyethylene and nylon, are long chains of carbon and hydrogen.

44
00:02:53.216 --> 00:02:58.196
They have low density, they flex, and they soften at modest temperatures.

45
00:02:58.616 --> 00:03:02.755
Composites combine two or more materials to get the best of each.

46
00:03:02.755 --> 00:03:05.457
Fiberglass is glass fibers in a polymer.

47
00:03:05.457 --> 00:03:09.079
Carbon fiber composites are stiff, strong and light.

48
00:03:09.999 --> 00:03:12.732
Your lecture compares them in one table.

49
00:03:12.732 --> 00:03:15.988
Ductility is good in metals and poor in ceramics.

50
00:03:15.988 --> 00:03:19.883
Electrical and thermal conductivity are high only in metals.

51
00:03:20.303 --> 00:03:23.375
Ceramics win on hardness and stiffness, but have

52
00:03:23.375 --> 00:03:26.566
low fracture toughness and are hard to machine.

53
00:03:26.566 --> 00:03:32.120
Polymers are low in stiffness, but machine easily and resist corrosion fairly well.

54
00:03:36.020 --> 00:03:40.447
Why study all this? Because getting the material wrong can be catastrophic.

55
00:03:40.447 --> 00:03:42.437
Your lecture lists real failures.

56
00:03:42.857 --> 00:03:47.006
Liberty ships in World War Two cracked in two in cold water.

57
00:03:47.006 --> 00:03:49.916
The cause was the ductile to brittle transition

58
00:03:49.916 --> 00:03:53.136
of B C C iron: cold steel became brittle.

59
00:03:53.556 --> 00:03:56.463
The Challenger shuttle, in nineteen eighty six.

60
00:03:56.463 --> 00:03:58.267
A polymer O-ring seal failed.

61
00:03:58.686 --> 00:04:01.865
The Hyatt Regency walkway in nineteen eighty one.

62
00:04:01.865 --> 00:04:05.214
Overstressed, under-designed steel support rods.

63
00:04:05.214 --> 00:04:07.711
One hundred and fourteen people died.

64
00:04:08.131 --> 00:04:13.163
The Alaska M D eighty crash, from excessive wear on a stabilizer jackscrew.

65
00:04:13.163 --> 00:04:16.792
The de Havilland Comet, the first commercial jet, from

66
00:04:16.792 --> 00:04:19.893
metal fatigue at rivet holes near the windows.

67
00:04:20.312 --> 00:04:25.277
The United D C ten at Sioux City, from an inclusion and cracking in an engine.

68
00:04:25.277 --> 00:04:28.400
And the Tacoma Narrows bridge, from poor design,

69
00:04:28.400 --> 00:04:31.022
with too little stiffening against wind.

70
00:04:31.442 --> 00:04:33.880
Every one of these comes back to something in this

71
00:04:33.880 --> 00:04:37.585
course: toughness, fatigue, wear, defects and design.

72
00:04:41.485 --> 00:04:43.361
Here is the summary of topic one.

73
00:04:43.782 --> 00:04:46.782
A material can be shaped into a product that has a function.

74
00:04:47.201 --> 00:04:49.456
Science links structure to properties.

75
00:04:49.456 --> 00:04:52.863
Engineering designs the structure for the properties we need.

76
00:04:53.284 --> 00:04:58.179
A property is the response to a stimulus, and it does not depend on shape or size.

77
00:04:58.599 --> 00:05:04.045
And each class, metals, ceramics, polymers and composites, has its own trade-offs.

78
00:05:04.045 --> 00:05:08.610
Next, we zoom in to the atom, and the bonds that hold materials together.
